2. IS THE ORACLE INDEPENDENT OF THE DRIVER?
  ........................................................
  Chain of custody for the value that decides pass/fail:

    kernel/src/hda.rs generate_sine_sample() -> 5 x 4096-byte DMA frames -> BDL
      -> HDA stream -> QEMU intel-hda -> hda-output -> `-audiodev wav,path=...`
      -> 16-bit PCM WAV -> tools/verify-tone.py (Goertzel) -> exit code
      -> kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh:122 `if ! ... verify-tone.py --expect 440`
      -> FAILED=1 -> test-boot.sh exit 1 -> scripts/check.sh:108
      -> `bash test-boot.sh 2>&1 | tee ... || FAILED=1` -> `check: FAILURES`

  Independence: yes.
    - tools/verify-tone.py imports argparse, math, os, struct, sys, wave only. No
      kernel constant, no shared table, no read of kernel/src/hda.rs. The expected
      value arrives as `--expect 440` from test-boot.sh, written separately from
      `pub const TONE_FREQ_HZ: u32 = 440`.
    - The measured signal is the audio QEMU produced from the guest's DMA buffer.
      A kernel that fills the buffer and never programs the stream yields silence
      or a missing WAV, both rejected.
    - check.sh runs `set -uo pipefail` (line 5), so stage 8's pipeline keeps
      test-boot.sh's exit status. I checked this specifically because stage 15 has
      the opposite defect (F2.10-1). Stage 8 is sound; stage 15 is not.

  What it does not prove:
    - It proves the emitted wave is 440 Hz. It does not prove the 4800-frame loop
      is seamless; the wrap phase error is 6.1e-7 cycle (~0.1 LSB), so it is.
    - It does not prove the guest's sample rate. The guest programs 48 kHz; the
      WAV is 44100 Hz because QEMU's wav backend resamples. docs/logs/k5-hda-output.log
      prints "48 kHz" and "rate=44100 Hz" in one block with no note of the
      resample. Finding F6.

