4. SENSITIVITY OF THE GATE ITSELF (rule 15: this one is MISSED)
  ........................................................
  tools/verify-tone.py is part of 3128d7f, and it is the only thing between a
  wrong tone and a green run. So I planted a fault in it: `return True` as the
  first line of verify_wav.

  $ grep -n "PLANT" tools/verify-tone.py
  116:    return True  # PLANT: oracle that cannot fail
  $ python tools/verify-tone.py battery/B_880_full.wav --expect 440; echo $?
  0
  $ git checkout -- tools/verify-tone.py
  $ python tools/verify-tone.py battery/B_880_full.wav --expect 440; echo $?
  1

  Nothing in the repository tests the tool:
  $ grep -rn "verify-tone" --include=*.sh --include=*.py --include=*.yml .
  ./kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh:122:        if ! "${PYCMD[@]}" "$REPO_ROOT/tools/verify-tone.py" "$AUDIO_WAV" --expect 440; then

  One caller, no test, no fixture. A change that desensitises the oracle is caught
  by nothing, so the plant that turns the oracle into `return True` is MISSED.
  This is the F2.10-1 failure mode in a different costume: the gate is a single
  unguarded file. Finding F1, blocking.

5. WHAT THE SELFTEST CHECKS COVER
  ........................................................
  $ git show 3128d7f:kernel/src/hdaselftest.rs | grep -c "    check("
  9
  After a3dd44f, the same count is 10; CI prints `NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 1 of 10
  hda-gate checks failed` on plant P2, which agrees.

  At 3128d7f the nine checks are four sine-math checks (phase 0, pi/2, pi, 3pi/2)
  and five device checks. Two of the device checks are the same predicate: check 1
  asserts init() returned Ok, check 5 asserts is_ready(), and is_ready() is
  `c.active`, a flag init() sets. is_ready() never reads LPIB, so check 5 cannot
  tell a cycling stream from a stalled one, despite the name "DMA engine running
  with cyclic 440 Hz tone buffer". a3dd44f adds check 6 for exactly that.

6. THE FLAKE AS IT WAS, FROM THE RUN LOGS
  ........................................................
  $ gh run view 41624640686 --log-failed | grep "audio too short"
  FAIL: audio too short: 1330 samples at 44100 Hz     (SeaBIOS/selftest)
  FAIL: audio too short: 1329 samples at 44100 Hz     (OVMF/selftest)
  $ gh run view 41630744368 --log-failed | grep "audio too short"
  FAIL: audio too short: 1770 samples at 44100 Hz     (SeaBIOS/selftest)
  FAIL: audio too short: 1770 samples at 44100 Hz     (OVMF/selftest)
  $ gh run view 42251306606 --log-failed | grep "audio too short"
  FAIL: audio too short: 1328 samples at 44100 Hz     (SeaBIOS/selftest)
  FAIL: audio too short: 1328 samples at 44100 Hz     (OVMF/selftest)

  1328/1329/1330 samples are 30.1 ms; 1770 is 40.1 ms. QEMU's wav backend writes
  in ~441-sample (10 ms) chunks, so these are 3.0 and 4.0 chunks. Passing runs in
  docs/logs/k5-hda-output.log recorded 5678 and 6285 samples (129/143 ms). The
  flaking files are truncated, not mistuned. That is consistent with the guest
  exiting a variable few audio ticks after the stream starts. It does not, by
  itself, prove the commit's "< 40 ms" diagnosis (guest lifetime versus late
  stream start are indistinguishable from the sample counts alone); the fix makes
  the distinction moot, which is why I do not treat the diagnosis as false.

7. A2.20 REVIEW: DOES THE FIX REMOVE THE RACE OR ONLY NARROW IT?
  ........................................................
  The fix (a3dd44f, kernel/src/hda.rs): wait_stream_cycles(cycles) polls SDnLPIB
  at sd_base+0x04, counts a wraparound when the position falls from >9600 to
  <9600, and returns the count, bounded by a 20,000,000-iteration timeout. The new
  check 6 asserts `cycles >= 2`, i.e. 200 ms of audio, which is 8820 samples at
  44.1 kHz. The wait is a counted number of complete buffer passes, not a sleep,
  so it removes the race rather than making it rarer — provided LPIB advances.

  The author's two claimed runs, read directly:

  $ gh run view 42263125407 --json headBranch,status,conclusion
  plant/k5c-tone-10x completed success
  $ gh run view 42263125407 --log | grep -c "PASS: 440.0 Hz tone verified"
  22
  $ ... grep -c "PASS (SeaBIOS/selftest)"  -> 11
  $ ... grep -c "PASS (OVMF/selftest)"     -> 11

  So the loop ran 11 iterations, not 10, and printed 22 tone passes, not 20. The
  commit message says "10/10 ... 20/20". The substance holds (11/11 pairs green,
  9,284 to 9,293 samples = 210 ms, which is 2 cycles plus exit latency), but the
  displayed counts do not match the run. Finding F5.

  $ gh run view 42260150614 --json headBranch,status,conclusion
  plant/k5c-880hz completed failure
    verify-tone: detected=880.0 Hz ... samples=9746
    FAIL: detected peak frequency 880.0 Hz differs from expected 440.0 Hz ...
    FAIL: SeaBIOS/selftest tone verification failed
    (and the same for OVMF/selftest)

  My own plants, both pushed from a3dd44f on this reviewer's branches:

  P1  plant/f2.11-880  `TONE_FREQ_HZ: u32 = 440 -> 880`, run 42265842808: FAILURE
    $ gh run view 42265842808 --log-failed | grep -E "detected|FAIL \("
    verify-tone: target=440.0 Hz, detected=880.0 Hz, ... samples=9284
    FAIL: detected peak frequency 880.0 Hz differs from expected 440.0 Hz by 440.0 Hz (tolerance: 10.0 Hz)
    FAIL (SeaBIOS/selftest)
    FAIL (OVMF/selftest)
    check: FAILURES (see above)
    Note samples=9284: the 2-cycle wait is in effect on this branch, the capture is
    210 ms long, and the gate still rejects the wrong tone. Both halves work
    together.

  P2  plant/f2.11-wait  wraparound threshold `prev_pos > 9600 && cur_pos < 9600`
      -> `> 20000 && < 20000` (unreachable, LPIB wraps at 19200), run 42265860293: FAILURE
    $ gh run view 42265860293 --log | grep -E "hda/dma|NOVA_SELFTEST"
      ok    hda/dma: Output stream DMA engine running with cyclic 440 Hz tone buffer
      FAIL  hda/dma: stream completed at least 2 full buffer cycles (>= 200 ms audio DMA) - HDA DMA stream stalled or timed out before 2 cycles
    NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 1 of 10 hda-gate checks failed
    Check 6 reaches the exit code and stages 8's pipeline carries it: `check: FAILURES`.

  Finding F2: check 6's subject is the return value of wait_stream_cycles, the same
  function that produces it. It can fail (P2 proves that) but it cannot distinguish
  "the DMA wrapped twice" from "the wait function computed 2". A `return 2` would
  pass check 6; only the WAV length would notice, and only statistically. The
  stronger form would assert on stream_lpib() observed separately from the loop, or
  compare audio duration with cycles.

  Finding F4: audio capture and the WAV check are both keyed on `expect_rc == 33`
  in test-boot.sh (lines 104 and 120). If the selftest image's expected exit code
  is ever changed in the CASES table, the audio device is not attached and the tone
  check is not run — the case still passes at its new code, with no audio
  verification and no message saying so.

